  "articleBody": "It's super frustrating when your brand-new built-in shelves start to sag, right? This usually comes down to a few common culprits. Often, folks underestimate the weight capacity needed or don't anchor their shelves properly, especially if they're not hitting studs. Walls aren't always perfectly straight either, which can throw things off if you don't account for those out-of-square conditions. Maybe the material just wasn't quite strong enough for what you're storing, or perhaps the installation skipped a few key leveling and shimming steps. Sometimes, what looks simple can hide a lot of little details that make a big difference in the long run. If your shelves are noticeably sagging, it's a good idea to take everything off them and re-evaluate. You might need to add more support, ensure they're firmly attached to wall studs, or even reinforce the shelves themselves. For heavier loads, anchoring directly into studs is a must. If it feels like a bigger job than you're comfortable with, or if you're dealing with multiple units, getting a professional opinion can save you a lot of headache and ensure your shelves are safe and sturdy for years to come. Don't let those sagging shelves get you down—there's usually a fix!",
